{% extends 'shells/docs.html.twig' %}
{% block title %}Performance · php-via Docs{% endblock %}
{% block content %}
<article>
    <h1>Performance</h1>
    <p>
        A php-via server keeps a context in memory for every open tab and holds one SSE stream per tab.
        Its capacity depends on what each page view, open tab, action and broadcast costs, and those
        costs are listed below, measured on the code of this website. The full tables, and the earlier
        comparison of 0.13.0 with 0.13.1, are in
        <a href="https://github.com/mbolli/php-via/blob/master/bench/capacity/RESULTS.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">bench/capacity/RESULTS.md</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
        Measured on two physical cores of an Intel i5-13500 held at full clock, with one worker, PHP 8.5,
        OpenSwoole 26.2, opcache on and Brotli level 4. A virtual server's core is usually slower: scale
        the CPU figures as described in <a href="#measure">measuring your own app</a>. Memory figures carry over.
    </p>

    <h2 id="costs">What each part costs</h2>
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Unit</th><th>CPU</th><th>Memory</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>Page view (21 to 86&nbsp;KB of HTML)</td><td>1.3 to 4.2&nbsp;ms</td><td>14 to 37&nbsp;KB until the stream connects or 30&nbsp;s pass</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Opening a tab (page view plus stream)</td><td>2.4 to 4.6&nbsp;ms</td><td>see the next row</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Open tab, Brotli level 4</td><td>none while idle</td><td>570 to 650&nbsp;KB</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Open tab, Brotli level 1</td><td>none while idle</td><td>65 to 125&nbsp;KB</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Open tab, no Brotli</td><td>none while idle</td><td>40 to 75&nbsp;KB</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Private action with a <code>sync()</code></td><td>0.16 to 0.20&nbsp;ms</td><td></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Broadcast, per receiving tab</td><td>about 0.05&nbsp;ms</td><td></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Keep-alive comment every 15&nbsp;s</td><td>under 1% of a core for 1,000 tabs</td><td></td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
        A broadcast renders a cached view once per scope and then writes and compresses one frame per tab,
        so the per-tab cost is what grows with the audience. Broadcasts coalesce: a worker renders a busy
        scope at most once per tick (25&nbsp;ms by default), however many actions marked it.
        <a href="/docs/broadcasting#timing">Broadcasting</a> explains the timing.
    </p>

    <h2 id="tab-memory">Memory per open tab and the Brotli level</h2>
    <p>
        A tab's context and stream take around 40 to 75&nbsp;KB. With Brotli on, the stream's encoder
        adds a window of what it has compressed, which lets a patch that repeats earlier markup shrink to
        a few bytes. That window is what an open tab mostly costs:
    </p>
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Home page, 500 tabs, 20 broadcasts per second</th><th>Memory per tab</th><th>Bytes per tab per second</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>Brotli level 4 (default)</td><td>642&nbsp;KB</td><td>0.69&nbsp;KB</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Brotli level 1</td><td>121&nbsp;KB</td><td>10.03&nbsp;KB</td></tr>
            <tr><td>No Brotli</td><td>73&nbsp;KB</td><td>23.80&nbsp;KB</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
        The encoder grows with traffic: a busy stream, such as a live game board, levels off near 9&nbsp;MB
        at level 4 and 575&nbsp;KB at level 1. On a server short of memory and not of bandwidth, set
        <code>withBrotli(true, 1)</code>; the second argument is the level for responses and streams.
    </p>

    <h2 id="page-views">Page views that never open a stream</h2>
    <p>
        A crawler, a link preview or a prefetch loads the page but never connects its stream. php-via keeps
        that context until the connect timeout (<code>withContextConnectTimeout()</code>, 30&nbsp;s by
        default), so a crawler fetching 50 pages per second holds about 50&nbsp;MB. Before 0.13.1 such
        contexts, and every page that called <code>$c->scope()</code>, were never freed.
    </p>
    <p>
        When the contexts of such a burst expire, php-via destroys them in 10&nbsp;ms slices, and each one is
        freed as soon as nothing refers to it. Its internal helpers hold it weakly, so a destroyed context leaves
        no reference cycles for PHP's cycle collector, whose every run walks all live contexts. While the
        contexts of a 250,000-view burst expire, the collector runs once, where it ran 20 times when each
        destroyed context left some 10 objects in cycles. The longest pause left in such a burst, 0.33&nbsp;s,
        comes at its end from collector runs while the views arrive: they find nothing to free but still walk
        every live context.
    </p>

    <h2 id="broadcasts">Keeping broadcasts narrow</h2>
    <p>
        A broadcast to a page's scope re-renders the page and every component on it. Three patterns from this
        website keep a busy page cheap:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            Give a busy widget a scope of its own and broadcast to that scope. The home page counter lives in
            <code>home:counter</code>, so a click re-renders the counters and not the poll next to them.
            <a href="/docs/components#scoped-components">Components</a> shows the code.
        </li>
        <li>
            Put presence indicators in their own scope too. A broadcast to <code>Scope::GLOBAL</code> on
            every connect and disconnect re-renders every open page: with 1,000 readers open, each visitor
            arriving and leaving cost 506&nbsp;ms of CPU. With the indicator in its own scope and static pages
            rendered once, it costs 5&nbsp;ms.
        </li>
        <li>
            Render a static page once. A view that returns an empty string on updates sends nothing on the
            SSE connect or on a broadcast, and its components still patch themselves:
        </li>
    </ul>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$app->page('/docs/install', function (Context $c): void {
    $c->view(
        fn (bool $isUpdate): string => $isUpdate ? '' : $c->render('docs/install.html.twig'),
        cacheUpdates: false, // the update cache is keyed by scope; keep the empty render out of it
    );
});
{% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h2 id="workers">More workers</h2>
    <p>
        With <code>withWorkerNum(2)</code> on two cores, page views per second rose from 797 to 1,390 for
        <code>/docs/signals</code> and from 238 to 468 for <code>/docs/api</code>. Scoped signals, session data and the client list are shared between
        workers, and the shared context directory has to be sized for your traffic:
        <a href="/docs/deployment#same-machine">Deployment</a> gives the rule.
    </p>

    <h2 id="small-server">A small server, worked through</h2>
    <p>
        This website runs on a two-core virtual server with 2&nbsp;GB of memory. With about 1.2&nbsp;GB left
        for php-via after the system and the reverse proxy:
    </p>
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Load</th><th>Estimate</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>Open docs tabs, Brotli level 4</td><td>about 2,200</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Open docs tabs, Brotli level 1</td><td>about 19,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Crawler traffic</td><td>50 pages per second hold about 50&nbsp;MB</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Visitors arriving and leaving with 1,000 readers open</td><td>about 200 per second per core</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Clicks on a shared widget with 2,000 viewers</td><td>about 10 per second per core</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>The CPU rows assume a core as fast as the reference machine's; scale them as described below.</p>

    <h2 id="measure">Measuring your own app</h2>
    <p>
        The harness in
        <a href="https://github.com/mbolli/php-via/tree/master/bench/capacity" target="_blank" rel="noopener">bench/capacity</a>
        opens tabs, fires actions and shared clicks at fixed rates, sends visitors through, and reads the
        server's CPU and memory from <code>/proc</code>. Its README shows how to pin the server and the
        harness to separate cores and hold the server's clock. To compare a server with the reference machine, run
        <code>php -d opcache.enable_cli=1 bench/capacity/calibrate.php</code> on it: the reference prints
        <code>brotli 7 ms, php 60 ms</code>, and the ratio of your figures to these scales the CPU costs above.
    </p>
</article>
{% endblock %}
